  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mfenced><mi>WA</mi></mfenced></munder><mi>OOH</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Na</mi><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mfenced><mi>SB</mi></mfenced></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mfenced><mi>Salt</mi></mfenced></munder><mi>OONa</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/eecde211-6ebc-4ffb-bb0a-24ba0a830ed7-9894268_4.PNG\" style=\"width: 223px; height: 160px;\" /></p><p>&nbsp;Initially the conductance is low due to the feeble ionisation of acetic acid. On the addition of base, there is decrease in conductance(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>) not only due to the replacement of<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math> but also suppresses the dissociation of benzoic acid due to common ion benzoate. But very soon, the conductance increases(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>) on adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi></math> neutralises the un-dissociated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mi>COOH</mi></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mi>COONa</mi></math> which is the strong electrolyte. This increase in conductance continues raise up to the equivalence point. The graph near the equivalence point is curved due the hydrolysis of salt <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mi>COONa</mi></math>. Beyond the equivalence point, conductance increases(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></math>) more rapidly with the addition of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi></math> due to the highly conducting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>OH</mi><mo>&#8722;</mo></msup></math> ions</p>",
